  "client_view_text": " The write-ahead log is a set of records, each of which represents an operation that occurred within the database. Each record is written to the log before the corresponding operation is applied to the main database. This way, if the database crashes before all records in the log have been written to the main database, the database can recover by first writing all remaining records in the log to the main database and then applying the operations represented by the log records in the order they were written. This process ensures that the data in the main database is consistent with the data in the log, and thus the database can recover to a state that was consistent with the It maintains a record of all of its transactions, called a log, which is written to a disk before the changes are made to the database. This means that even if the database crashes, the log is preserved, and the database can recover from the point of recovery by replaying the transactions in the log. This is a technique often used in enterprise databases.\n\nWhen the database is shut down normally, the log is cleared, ensuring that no transaction is replayed multiple times. If the database crashes, the log remains intact, and the recovery process can begin. The database scans the log file to identify the last committed transaction, which marks the beginning of the recovery process. It then replays all the transactions from the log file until it reaches the point of the last committed transaction. This ensures that the database is restored to a consistent state, reflecting all actions that were either committed or rolled back. The log also enables the database to recover from partial failures, such as a loss of power or a software failure.\n\n## Step 1: Understand the concept of a write-ahead log\nA write-ahead log is a technique used in databases to maintain a record of all transactions before they are applied to the database. This record, called the log, is written to a disk before the changes"
